W("Write a paragraph of 80-100 words: 'If the tiger could speak to the visitors, what would he say?'", 4, "Open-ended. A model: 'I am not here for your amusement. In my forest I would hunt in the long grass and move in the dark. Here I walk a few steps and turn. Do not stare; look at the sky and understand why I stare at it.' Marks: ideas 2, link to the poem 1, language 1.", "H"),
W("Critically appreciate the poem in about 100 words.", 5, "Model: Leslie Norris's 'A Tiger in the Zoo' contrasts a free and a captive tiger. The poet uses vivid imagery, alliteration and repetition ('quiet', 'brilliant', 'should be') to build sympathy. The irregular rhyme and short lines suit the pacing animal. The tone is sad and quietly angry; the last image of stars gives a sense of longing for freedom. The poem makes us think about keeping wild animals in captivity. (content 3, language 2)", "H"),
