 ("table", ["Point", "Detail"], [["Title", "A Tiger in the Zoo"], ["Poet", "Leslie Norris (Welsh)"], ["Form", "Short poem in short lines; irregular rhyme (cage - rage, cars - stars)"], ["Speaker", "An observer, who watches and sympathises"], ["Setting", "A zoo at night, and, in imagination, the jungle"], ["Tone", "Sympathetic, sad, quietly critical"], ["Theme", "The cruelty of captivity; the tiger's lost freedom and wild nature"]]),
 ("h2", "2. Summary"),
 ("p", "The poem begins with a striking picture of the tiger stalking the few steps of his cage in his bright, vivid stripes. His paws are 'pads of velvet quiet', so he moves without a sound. But inside him there is 'quiet rage', anger that he does not or cannot express."),
 ("p", "Then the poet imagines what the tiger should be doing. He should be lurking in the shadows, sliding silently through long grass near a water hole where plump deer pass by. He should be snarling around the houses at the edge of the jungle, baring his white fangs and claws, and frightening the village."),
 ("p", "But the real picture is different. The tiger is locked in a concrete cell with his strength behind bars. He stalks the length of his cage and ignores the visitors. At night he hears the last voices and the patrolling cars (the guards on their rounds), and stares with his brilliant eyes at the brilliant stars: a silent sign of longing for the free, open sky."),
