 ("p", "The poem moves from a close-up of the cage to a wide shot of the jungle and back again, ending with a view of the night sky. The lines are short, so that the reader feels the tiger's constant, restricted pacing. The tone is sympathetic and sad, but not shrill; the poet leaves it to the images to move us. There is no direct accusation, but the contrast between the 'should be' and the 'is' implies criticism of captivity."),
